# RunicAI Tool Agent

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"},
  {:runic_ai, path: "~/wrk/runic_ai"}
])

Code.require_file("support/runic_ai_livebook.exs", __DIR__)
```

## What This Notebook Teaches

Tool calling is the point where a model can ask your application to do work.
RunicAI keeps that boundary explicit:

* `Tool.Definition` describes a capability to the model and to the workflow
* `Tool.Selection` chooses the visible tool set for the turn
* `Tool.ExecutionPlan` records what will be executed
* `Tool.CallResult` captures the actual application result
* `Tool.Observation` converts the result back into model-visible context

The recipe used here is `RunicAI.Recipes.ConversationalToolAgent`.

## Provider Setup

```elixir
setup = RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.inputs()
```

```elixir
model = RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.read_model!(setup)
llm_opts = RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.llm_opts(model, max_output_tokens: RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.read_max_output_tokens(setup))
```

## Define A Tool

The executor is ordinary Elixir. The schema is provider-facing and is validated
before the executor runs.

```elixir
alias RunicAI.Tool.Definition

weather_tool =
  Definition.new!(
    name: "get_weather",
    description: "Get a lightweight weather report for a city.",
    parameter_schema: [
      location: [type: :string, required: true]
    ],
    executor: fn %{location: location} ->
      {:ok,
       %{
         location: location,
         forecast: "sunny",
         temperature_c: 23,
         source: "local demo weather service"
       }}
    end
  )

weather_tool
```

```elixir
single_tool_batch = RunicAI.Livebook.Tools.once_per_loop_selector()
```

```elixir
weather_tool |> Definition.to_req_llm_tool!()
```

## Visualize The Tool Agent Workflow

```elixir
alias RunicAI.Recipes.ConversationalToolAgent

tool_workflow =
  ConversationalToolAgent.new(
    model: model,
    registry: [weather_tool],
    tool_selector: single_tool_batch,
    tool_requirement: :required,
    llm_opts: llm_opts
  )

RunicAI.Livebook.WorkflowViz.tabs(tool_workflow)
```

## Run A Full Tool Loop

`react_until_resolution/3` runs the initial model turn, executes any requested
tools, appends observations, and submits the next model turn until the agent
answers or stops.

```elixir
RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.require_openai_key!()

tool_run =
  ConversationalToolAgent.react_until_resolution(
    [
      model: model,
      registry: [weather_tool],
      tool_selector: single_tool_batch,
      tool_requirement: :required,
      llm_opts: llm_opts
    ],
    %{user: "What is the weather in Paris? Use the weather tool."}
  )

ConversationalToolAgent.latest_reply_text(tool_run)
```

```elixir
Kino.Layout.tabs(
  "Tool selection": Kino.Tree.new(ConversationalToolAgent.latest_selection(tool_run)),
  "Execution plan": Kino.Tree.new(ConversationalToolAgent.latest_execution_plan(tool_run)),
  "Tool results": Kino.Tree.new(ConversationalToolAgent.latest_tool_results(tool_run)),
  Observations: Kino.Tree.new(ConversationalToolAgent.latest_observations(tool_run)),
  State: Kino.Tree.new(ConversationalToolAgent.latest_state(tool_run))
)
```

## Interactive Tool Chat

Try asking for weather in different cities. The widget renders model replies
and tool batches as workflow events.

```elixir
RunicAI.Livebook.Chat.start(
  ConversationalToolAgent,
  [
    model: model,
    registry: [weather_tool],
    tool_selector: single_tool_batch,
    tool_requirement: :required,
    llm_opts: llm_opts
  ],
  initial_message: "Check the weather in Lisbon and explain what you used."
)
```

## Design Notes

RunicAI does not make tools magical. A tool call is model output until the
workflow turns it into a `Tool.CallRequest`, plans it, executes it, and appends
an observation. That gives you places to add policies, audit logs, retries,
operator review, and per-tool visibility.
